# Secrets. Never relax this line.
.env

# Environments and bytecode
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.egg-info/
node_modules/
app/web/dist/

# Regenerable derived data.
#
# .cache is ~70MB of embedding cache keyed on content_hash + PARSER_VERSION. It
# is a speed optimisation, not an input: deleting it costs API calls, not
# correctness.
.cache/
data/eval/judged_partial.json
experiments/out/
.qdrant/

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DELIBERATELY TRACKED, against the usual instinct: data/raw/ and data/index/
#
# Both were ignored, and the consequence was that a fresh clone had no corpus and
# no embeddings -- so the first thing a reader had to do was supply their own
# Gemini key and wait ~20 minutes for an ingest before anything worked at all.
# For a repository whose whole purpose is to be read and run by someone else,
# that is a defect, not tidiness.
#
#   data/raw/    17MB, public arXiv papers plus the DOCX/image test files.
#                Redistribution is fine, and the eval numbers are meaningless
#                without the exact corpus they were measured on.
#   data/index/  6.5MB of vectors and chunk metadata. This is what lets the
#                container answer a question on first boot.
#   data/eval/   the golden set and judged results -- the evidence for every
#                number in the README. Was already tracked; correctly so.
#
# 24MB total, well inside GitHub's limits. The alternative -- a fetch script --
# exists (scripts/fetch_corpus.py) but cannot reproduce the index without a key,
# and cannot guarantee the papers are still at the same URLs.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Editor and tooling noise
.idea/
.vscode/

# .claude holds BOTH kinds of file, so it cannot be ignored wholesale -- the same
# runtime-versus-build distinction that had to be untangled under data/index/.
#
# CLAUDE.md is project configuration: instructions every collaborator should get,
# and ignoring it means the next person works to different rules and nothing says
# so. The lock file is a running process's state.
.claude/*
!.claude/CLAUDE.md

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RUNTIME STATE under data/index/ -- tracked by accident, now excluded.
#
# data/index/ was deliberately un-ignored so the prebuilt index ships and a fresh
# clone can answer a question immediately. That was right, and it captured more
# than intended: `conversations/` and `jobs/` live under the same directory and
# are RUNTIME USER STATE, not build output. Eight of my ad-hoc debugging
# conversations ("drift test") and nine job records were staged for a public repo.
#
# The distinction is one the code already makes -- conversations and jobs are the
# only two runtime subdirectories of DATA_INDEX -- and the two kinds should never
# have shared a durability decision. On a customer deployment these files are
# THEIR data.
#
# Widening an exemption captured more than it named: the same shape as widening a
# guard and invalidating a string written while it was narrow.
data/index/conversations/
data/index/jobs/
# Derived from the conversations above, so it cannot be tracked when they are not
# -- it would ship describing turns that no longer exist in the repo. Rebuilt
# automatically on first read.
data/index/citation_index.json

# Scratch API response dumps from manual testing
/t[0-9]*.json

# Untrusted uploads are inspected here before admission; never committed.
data/quarantine/
